 15: /**
 16:  * Grass that has been set on fire, and the fire still doing it.
 17:  *
 18:  * Two things in one file because they are one thing: the fires are the only
 19:  * writer of the scar, and the scar is the only record the fires leave.
 20:  *
 21:  * A scar is not round and it is not bounded. A fight that moves leaves a
 22:  * wandering burnt path, and a fire that spreads leaves a blot with a shape of
 23:  * its own, so this is `snowTrail`'s world-space map rather than `snowMelt`'s
 24:  * pool of circles: one byte per texel, stamped as the fire passes, uploaded
 25:  * over a dirty rectangle rather than whole, and decayed by a pass that walks
 26:  * only what has been painted.
 27:  *
 28:  * A leaf, on purpose. `terrainOverlay.ts` imports `snowMelt.ts` and
 29:  * `snowCover.ts` imports `terrainOverlay.ts`, and naming another weather
 30:  * module at module scope from inside that cycle has already crashed the client
 31:  * on load once (`snowMelt.ts:33-43`). Nothing here imports the grass; the
 32:  * grass imports this, and hands its own "does anything grow at this point"
 33:  * back through `setGrassProbe`.
 34:  *
 35:  * Local, and it does not pretend otherwise. Two players watching the same
 36:  * fireball see different scars. A burn is a function of what somebody did
 37:  * rather than of the clock, so the `ambientSchedule` trick - world state as a
 38:  * pure function of `serverNow()` - does not reach it, and the protocol carries
 39:  * no timestamp for an impact today (`serverTime.ts:9-11`). `snowMelt` and
 40:  * `snowTrail` are both local for the same reason.
 41:  */
 42: 
 43: // ---- 1. tuning -------------------------------------------------------------
 44: 
 45: /**
 46:  * Texels per tile. Four puts a texel at 25 cm, finer than a blade is tall.
 47:  * The edge gets its raggedness from the per-blade hash in the shader, not from
 48:  * this, so there is nothing to buy by going higher: 1024x1024 is one megabyte
 49:  * against `snowTrail`'s four.
 50:  */
 51: export const BURN_RES = 4;
 52: 
 53: /** Side of the burn map in texels. */
 54: export const BURN_SIZE = 256 * BURN_RES;
 55: 
 56: /**
 57:  * Seconds for a full burn to grow back. The ask was "at least five minutes",
 58:  * and `snowTrail` next door has been running a 240-second fill on the same
 59:  * machinery, so this is a proven shape at a longer setting.
 60:  */
 61: const REGROW_SECONDS = 300;
 62: 
 63: /**
 64:  * Seconds between decay passes; each walks the painted rectangle.
 65:  *
 66:  * One rather than two, so the freshness the flame front reads off this map
 67:  * steps about every 1.2 s instead of every 2.4. It is the resolution of the
 68:  * burning band, and it costs a second walk of a rectangle that is a few
 69:  * hundred texels.
 70:  */
 71: const DECAY_EVERY = 1;
 72: 
 73: /** Fires alight at once. Past a handful they overlap into one blaze anyway. */
 74: const MAX_FIRES = 6;
 75: 
 76: /**
 77:  * How fast a front travels at full vigour, in tiles a second, and how fast
 78:  * spreading costs it that vigour.
 79:  *
 80:  * The second is the whole of "it should die down the more it spreads": vigour
 81:  * falls at `radius / VIGOUR_SPAN`, so a fire that has got somewhere is a fire
 82:  * that is going out, and the further it has got the faster it goes out. A
 83:  * fireball's 1.2-tile hit reaches about four tiles before it gives up;
 84:  * Hellfire's 2.6 reaches further because it starts further, with no special
 85:  * case anywhere to say so.
 86:  *
 87:  * Both were roughly twice this, which burnt the whole patch out in about three
 88:  * seconds - the fire was over before the eye found it, and what was left was a
 89:  * black shape that had simply appeared. Half the rate against nearly three
 90:  * times the span covers the same ground over about ten seconds, so the front
 91:  * is something a player can watch cross the field.
 92:  */
 93: const SPREAD_RATE = 0.55;
 94: const VIGOUR_SPAN = 26;
 95: 
 96: /** Past this a front is done however much vigour is left. */
 97: const FIRE_MAX_RADIUS = 7;
 98: 
 99: /** Vigour lost per second where the ring finds nothing to burn. */
100: const STARVE_RATE = 2.5;
101: 
102: /** Points sampled around a ring to ask whether there is still fuel. */
103: const FUEL_SAMPLES = 8;
104: 
105: /**
106:  * Seconds between ember bursts from one front, and sparks a burst.
107:  *
108:  * Per front, which is what makes the rate matter: six fires at four sparks
109:  * every eighth of a second is two hundred a second, and at any distance that
110:  * stops being embers and becomes a pale haze hanging over the field. The
111:  * texture and the size were right all along - it was the count.
112:  */
113: const EMBER_EVERY = 0.22;
114: const EMBER_SPARKS = 1;
115: 
116: /**
117:  * Bearings lit per burst, spread evenly round the rim.
118:  *
119:  * The count is what stops the ring being a dotted line at a large radius and
120:  * a bonfire at a small one, so it is paid per fire and the interval above is
121:  * set against it: five points every 0.22 s is about twenty-two flames a
122:  * second from one front, against two hundred when the rate was tuned for a
123:  * single point.
124:  */
125: const RIM_POINTS = 5;
126: 
127: /**
128:  * The ember off burning grass.
129:  *
130:  * `FIRE_SPARKS` is the skills' own, sized for a fireball going off at chest
131:  * height, and at a third of a tile it lies in the grass like an orange brick.
132:  * Same texture and the same colours, a fifth of the size, and it falls rather
133:  * than flying: this comes off a blade, not out of an explosion.
134:  */
135: const GRASS_EMBERS: ParticleRecipe = {
136:   ...FIRE_SPARKS,
137:   size: 0.03,
138:   sizeJitter: 0.012,
139:   life: 0.9,
140:   power: 0.7,
141:   gravity: -1.2,
142: };
143: 
144: /**
145:  * How far the fire reaches off a perfect circle, per harmonic.
146:  *
147:  * The second harmonic gives a front a long axis, the third and fifth break
148:  * that into lobes. Together they run the reach over roughly 0.57 to 1.43 of
149:  * the nominal radius, which is enough that no two scars look alike and not so
150:  * much that a fire tears into separate pieces.
151:  */
152: const LOBE_2 = 0.22;
153: const LOBE_3 = 0.13;
154: const LOBE_5 = 0.08;
155: 
156: /** The most `reach` can return; the stamp box has to allow for it. */
157: const MAX_REACH = 1 + LOBE_2 + LOBE_3 + LOBE_5;
158: 
159: /** How black a texel goes when the fire passes over it. */
160: const CHAR = 255;
